 did was clumsy  it was not done with a lot of grace  i was under the impression of certain  things that happened that hadn't  happened  and but  i want to make it clear  france is an extreme  extremely valuable  for his part macron is ready to move  forward  i believe that this was after our two  exchanges in september and october an  important meeting because following the  acas affair  we have initiated real common work and a  political response and reinforce  cooperation between the united states of  america and france  both sides hope the rebuilding of trust  will help bring about the change these  young people are marching for  and for more on this let's talk to dw  correspondent alexandra von naman who's  at the g20 summit in rome alexandra it's  going to last two days what's on the  agenda today  the leaders today are going to talk  about the coronavirus pandemic and they  will talk about ways to accelerate  vaccine production and their  distribution they are expected to back  the goal of vaccinated vaccinating 70  percent of the world population by mid  2022 however we have to say that as for  now only barely 50  of the world population have received  one jab so this is a very difficult  topic another topic that they will be  talking about is the global economy and  we expect some leaders to raise the  issue of soaring energy prices and  disruptions to global supply chains and  the leaders are also expected to endorse  a minimum corporate tax rate of 15  and that is going as they hope to lead  to billions or in additional revenues  and that that is that decision is  already being described as a once in a  generation achievement  and alexander when it comes to the  environment the u.n secretary general  antonio guterres demanded a signal from  the g20 leaders ahead of the u.n climate  summit in glasgow where they're heading  afterwards uh is that likely to happen  well we have to say that there is  actually no reason to be optimistic that  the g20 leaders will really pave the way  to the un conference in glasgow by  showing that there are committed to more  ambitious climate goals they are still  negotiating whether in the end the g20  will agree  to be  carbon neutral by 2050 and we are  already hearing from some countries for  example from china that they are saying  well we are rejecting calls for more  ambitious goals and when we take when we  look at the u.s for example joe biden's  climate plan is still still in congress  so at the moment unfortunately we have  to say that there is no reason to be  very optimistic  okay and there are a lot of  the main g20 leaders but xi jinping the  leader of china the world's biggest co2  producer is not attending in person just  over video link it's the same situation  for russia's president vladimir putin  does that all make it harder to get a  deal  yes of course because china and russia  are so important players when it comes  to their climate crisis but also when it  comes to other topics such as for  example the soaring energy prices it  would be good for all g20 leaders to sit  together and have a face-to-face  conference  conversation that confrontation sorry  but this is not going to happen and it's  really we have to lower our expectations  because those two leaders putin and she  are not attending the summit in person  yeah a bit of a freudian slip and i  think we can well understand why uh  alexander fondament thanks so much for  that
 

